      "problem": "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,+\\infty ).$\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- It has no zeros and no global extrema.\n\n- The branches of the function alternate between positive and negative, with a positive branch in $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},….$\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254588/assets/9911289de363d082a34f3be743990dea3b76315ae2b3ddd41bccc6e3797840d2.png)",
